Block AWhat does compounding actually do?More often always helps, by less each time; the ceiling is e^{r}


Block BWhich of two quotes is cheaper?Compare on EAR = (1 + r/n)^{n} - 1, never on the nominal rate


Block CWhat is a future euro worth today?Discount before adding: PV = FV/(1+r)^{t}


Block DBuild the line or not?NPV = +€319k at 9% — accept; IRR is where NPV crosses zero


Today the same arithmetic prices a bond, and a derivative tells you what a rate rise costs.

The second derivative, in one line
f'' &gt; 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{convex, holds water, a minimum} f'' &lt; 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{concave, spills water, a maximum}
An inflection is where f'' changes sign: the curve stops bending one way and starts bending the other. In business that is where growth stops accelerating and begins to slow — the moment worth naming in a report.

A surplus is an area, and an area is an integral
The price rises from 40 to 50. On every unit still bought, consumers pay €10 more; on the units they stop buying, they lose what the purchase was worth to them. Added up, that is the area to the left of the demand curve:
\Delta CS = \int_{40}^{50} (100 - p)\,dp = \Big[100p - \tfrac{p^{2}}{2}\Big]_{40}^{50} = 3750 - 3200 = \mathbf{550}

\Delta PS = \int_{40}^{50} (p - 20)\,dp = \Big[\tfrac{p^{2}}{2} - 20p\Big]_{40}^{50} = 250 - 0 = \mathbf{250} \qquad \text{tariff revenue} = 10 \times 20 = \mathbf{200}
The week-2 integral, put to work: the integral of a schedule between two prices is the money that changed hands.

Conditional probability: a cell over a total
P(A \mid B) = \frac{\text{number in both } A \text{ and } B}{\text{number in } B}
“Given B” means: look only at B’s row (or column), and ask what share of it is A. Everything else in the table is ignored.


Same cell, 14, on top.
Given Volta, faulty? Look only at Volta’s row, 700 batteries:
P(F \mid \text{Volta}) = \frac{14}{700} = \textbf{.02}

Given faulty, Volta? Look only at the faulty column, 29 batteries:
P(\text{Volta} \mid F) = \frac{14}{29} = \textbf{.48}


Two different questions, two different totals underneath. Say which total before you divide.
